      children: "This chapter is a practical bridge between the gunas of chapter fourteen and the faith of chapter seventeen. The divine endowment is a sattva-dominant nature expressing itself in action; the demonic endowment is rajas and tamas in their raw, unexamined forms. Notice also the thread of the karma-bhakti-jnana frame: the daivi qualities make karma yoga possible (you act without craving), they open the heart for bhakti (freedom from fear is the beginning of love), and they clear the mirror for jnana (purity of heart precedes the vision of the Self). Chapter sixteen is where the whole Gita becomes personal: it asks, not \"what is the truth?\" but \"what is happening in you?\""
